    "file": "src/adss/das.py",
    "line": 120,
    "summary": "The generated clock check compares a UTC partition date against a session-timezone cast of a TIMESTAMPTZ, so it fails on any machine that is not on UTC.",
    "failure_scenario": "REPRODUCED. `extracted_at` is `to_timestamp(...)`, which DuckDB types as TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE; `cast(... AS DATE)` therefore resolves in the session timezone, while `extracted_on` is the dlt layout's UTC date. The committed warehouse loaded at 2026-09-07 21:52 UTC: `TZ=Asia/Tokyo uv run adss check` -> `FAIL orders__clock: nothing disagrees / 830 disagreements`, exit 1 (UTC: PASS). `TZ=Asia/Tokyo pytest tests/test_das_executes.py` fails the same way at test_the_observation_clock_agrees_with_the_partition. Fix: compare `cast(timezone('UTC', staged.extracted_at) AS DATE)`."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/adss/das.py",
    "line": 130,
    "summary": "key_check_sql emits `count(DISTINCT a, b)`, which DuckDB rejects, so any contract with a composite primary key produces an unrunnable check.",
    "failure_scenario": "REPRODUCED against DuckDB: `SELECT count(*) - count(DISTINCT t.x, t.y) FROM t` -> `BinderException: No function matches the given name and argument types 'count(INTEGER, VARCHAR)'`. A contract declaring `primary_keys: [order_id, line_id]` makes `adss das unpack` write that into `checks/<t>__key.sql` (sqlfluff parses it fine, so nothing catches it at generation), and `adss check` then raises the BinderException out of `_rows`, aborting the whole run so *no* findings are printed. The working form is already used in this same PR at checks.py:97: parenthesise the key list -> `count(DISTINCT (latest.a, latest.b))` (verified to work)."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/adss/contract.py",
    "line": 165,
    "summary": "read_contract never checks that target names are unique or that they do not collide with the layer's own provenance columns, and DuckDB silently renames duplicates rather than erroring.",
    "failure_scenario": "REPRODUCED: `CREATE VIEW v AS SELECT 1 AS name, 2 AS name` succeeds in DuckDB and yields columns `name`, `name_1` — no error. A source field `SourceUrl` snake_cases mechanically to `source_url` (the rule at contract.py:140 *forces* that name), so staged_view_sql emits `payload ->> '$.SourceUrl' AS source_url` and then `landed.source_url AS source_url`; the real provenance column becomes `source_url_1`. current_view_sql (das.py:87) then emits `staged.source_url AS source_url` twice, so the current view carries the payload field under both names and the actual source URL is unreachable — Blueprint S4 provenance silently lost, no error anywhere. The same happens for two nested paths sharing a leaf (`Ship.Name` and `Bill.Name` both -> `name`), where the second column's data is silently replaced by the first's."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/adss/cli.py",
    "line": 150,
    "summary": "das unpack writes generated check files but never removes stale ones, so a removed or renamed contract leaves an orphan check that aborts `adss check`.",
    "failure_scenario": "Rename `das/contracts/orders.yaml` to `sales.yaml` and run `adss build`. `checks/sales__clock.sql` and `checks/sales__key.sql` are written, but `checks/orders__clock.sql` and `checks/orders__key.sql` remain. `run_checks` globs `checks/*.sql` (checks.py:58) and executes every file, so the first orphan raises `duckdb.CatalogException: Table with name orders does not exist` out of `_rows`, killing the whole check run before any finding is printed. `source.record()` (source.py:64) already establishes the right pattern — delete the previous generation before writing — and this generator does not."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/adss/checks.py",
    "line": 110,
    "summary": "run_checks executes every question regardless of its declared status, so a draft, red or superseded question fails the build.",
    "failure_scenario": "`Question.status` is parsed and validated into a four-member enum (question.py:28-32, 67) and then read by nothing in src/ (grep confirms zero consumers). Mark a question `status: superseded` — the state the enum exists to express — and `adss check` still runs both its queries; if the superseded question's SQL references a peripheral that a later slice regenerated away, `adss check` raises a CatalogException and CI fails on a question nobody is supposed to be answering any more. Same for `status: draft` with unfinished SQL, which also breaks `destinations/app.py:83`, where `answers` executes every question's `uss_sql` eagerly."
  },
