  },
  {
    "file": "src/adss/uss.py",
    "line": 203,
    "summary": "bridge_columns orders the entity-key columns by event declaration order, not model order, so reordering dab/uss.yaml silently rearranges the published column contract.",
    "failure_scenario": "`dict.fromkeys(model.entity(event.entity_id).key_column for event in uss.events)` takes first-appearance order across *events*. The published contract in .claude/skills/dar/SKILL.md says `<entity>_key | one per entity, in model order`, and bridge_columns' own docstring says 'A model change appends; it never rearranges'. With entities A and B, moving an event on A above an existing event on B in uss.yaml — not a model change at all — flips the bridge from `b_key, a_key` to `a_key, b_key`. `run_checks` cannot catch it because the check derives `expected` from the same function, so both sides move together; every consumer written against the documented order breaks silently. Order the keys by `model.entities` instead of by `uss.events`."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/adss/destination.py",
    "line": 38,
    "summary": "shim uses `link.exists()` to decide whether to create the symlink, but that is False for a dangling link, so symlink_to raises FileExistsError.",
    "failure_scenario": "REPRODUCED: for a symlink whose target is gone, `Path.exists()` returns False while `Path.symlink_to()` raises `FileExistsError: [Errno 17]`. `.browsers/` is a persisted, gitignored cache. After the image is rebuilt or playwright is bumped, `/opt/pw-browsers/chromium_headless_shell-<old>` disappears while `.browsers/chromium_headless_shell-<build>/chrome-headless-shell-linux64/chrome-headless-shell` still points at it; the next `adss shoot` that needs the shim dies with an unhandled FileExistsError instead of re-pointing it. Both branches have the bug (lines 38 and 47), and `test_the_shim_is_idempotent` only exercises the live-target case. Guard with `link.is_symlink() or link.exists()` and unlink first."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/adss/platform.py",
    "line": 77,
    "summary": "`reading()` does not translate duckdb.IOException into WarehouseBusyError, so the one message this module exists to produce never fires on the read path.",
    "failure_scenario": "REPRODUCED: opening a DuckDB file `read_only=True` while another process holds it read-write raises `IOException: Could not set lock on file ...: Conflicting lock is held`. `exclusive()` wraps exactly this in the BUSY message (platform.py:58-61); `reading()` does not. Run `adss check` (cli.py:240) in one shell while `adss build` holds the warehouse in another and you get a raw duckdb traceback instead of 'the warehouse is busy: another process has ... open', which is the entire point of the BUSY constant and of conventions §9."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/adss/mapping.py",
    "line": 106,
    "summary": "The M5 reaching/aggregate regexes are matched against the raw expression text, so a keyword inside a string literal refuses a legitimate row-local expression.",
    "failure_scenario": "`_REACHING` is `\\b(select|join|from|over|group\\s+by)\\b` with IGNORECASE. A conforming CASE over the row's own columns — `CASE WHEN status = 'handed over' THEN 'x' ELSE 'y' END`, or any literal containing 'from', 'join' or 'over' — matches inside the quoted string and `check_mapping` raises `M5 -- ... reaches beyond its row`. M5's own text (conventions §1.4) explicitly permits a CASE over the row's columns, so the gate refuses the mapping it was written to allow, and the only way past it is to reword the business data. Strip string literals before scanning."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/adss/cli.py",
    "line": 226,
    "summary": "dar build runs the whole generator, including the sqlfluff formatter, purely to obtain a list of filenames, then installs different bytes read from disk.",
    "failure_scenario": "`_generate(project)` formats every generated statement through `sqlfluff.fix` (measured at ~2.5s of formatter time per full pass, on top of ~2s import) and its SQL is then discarded — only `generated.keys()` is used, to build `ordered`. Line 232 installs `(project.dar_sql / name).read_text()`. So `adss dar build` pays the full generation cost and still installs whatever is on disk: a hand-edit to `dar/uss/order.sql` (which the file header forbids) is installed into the warehouse while the correct SQL was computed and thrown away one line earlier. Either install `generated[name]` — which makes 'nothing here is hand-edited' true by construction rather than by a CI `--check` — or derive the ordering from `project.dar_sql.glob('*.sql')` without invoking the formatter."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/adss/engine.py",
    "line": 42,
    "summary": "verify() slurps the entire 174 MB vendored binary into memory on every engine invocation instead of streaming the digest.",
    "failure_scenario": "MEASURED on the real binary: `hashlib.sha256(p.read_bytes())` takes 0.57s and pushes RSS from 15 MB to 185 MB; `hashlib.file_digest(f, 'sha256')` gives the identical digest in 0.44s with no allocation. `Engine.run` calls `verify()` on every invocation and `adss build` invokes the engine three times (install, deploy, execute), so each build takes three ~170 MB allocation spikes and ~1.7s of hashing for a file whose contents have not changed within the run — on a memory-capped CI runner that spike is the difference between passing and an OOM kill."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/test_generated_sql_lints.py",
    "line": 42,
    "summary": "emitters() is called once at collection time and again inside every parametrized test, so the whole generator plus formatter runs nine times per test session.",
    "failure_scenario": "MEASURED: `pytest tests/test_generated_sql_lints.py` takes 24.9s; one `emitters()` call takes 4.5s and formats all eight statements through `sqlfluff.fix`. `sorted(emitters())` in the parametrize decorator is call one, and `emitters()[name]` at line 45 repeats the entire eight-statement formatting run for each of the eight tests, using one result and discarding seven. This runs on every commit through the pre-commit `pytest` hook and in CI's gate job. Compute it once (module-level constant or `@functools.cache`) and the file drops to roughly one generation plus the eight subprocess lints."
  },
  {
    "file": ".github/workflows/ci.yml",
    "line": 87,
    "summary": "The committed generated checks under checks/ have no `--check` regeneration in the gate, which the conventions require of every committed generated artefact.",
    "failure_scenario": "docs/conventions.md §8 (added in this diff): 'Generated artefacts are **committed**, and a `--check` regeneration in the gate fails on any diff. The exception is an artefact that cannot be the same on two machines — DAS's view SQL ... which is generated at build time and gitignored'. `checks/orders__clock.sql` and `checks/orders__key.sql` are committed generated artefacts and are not that exception, yet only `adss dar generate --check` runs here; there is no `adss das unpack --check`. Edit `checks/orders__key.sql` by hand (against its own 'Do not edit' header), or change a contract's `primary_keys` without regenerating, and CI stays green: `adss build` silently rewrites the file inside the runner, `adss check` runs the rewritten copy, and the committed copy a reviewer reads stays wrong — exactly the 'second truth nothing compares against the model' the rule names."
